rules_version = '2';

/**
 * Storage rules — deny-all, because nothing uploads yet.
 *
 * BUILD_PROMPT_V2 §3 puts profile photos, proof media and transformation images in
 * Storage, and v2.1b shipped rules for all three. The rules shipped; the feature did
 * not. There is no `storageBucket` in `src/lib/firebase.ts`, no `firebase/storage`
 * import anywhere in `src`, no bucket on the Admin app, `npm run emulators` does not
 * start Storage, and proof media is still a base64 data URL inside the Firestore
 * document (D3, D33).
 *
 * So this is back to deny-all, for the reason D38 gave for `firestore.rules` during
 * v2.1a: a permissive placeholder is the kind of thing that survives to production,
 * and a deny-all cannot. `firebase.json` already points `storage` here, so this file
 * was one `firebase deploy` from live — it was never inert.
 *
 * What it was granting: `allow read: if signedIn()` on /users/{userId}/proofs/, which
 * let ANY signed-in coach read ANY other coach's evidence photos. The Firestore
 * `proofs` rule narrows the same evidence to two people — the coach it is about and
 * the upline who asked. The bytes were open wider than the document describing them,
 * and the bytes are the sensitive half.
 *
 * The defence written above that grant ("enumeration is not possible") answered the
 * wrong question twice. The attacker it admits is signed IN, and a signed-in client
 * needs no token: a constructed `ref()` plus `getBytes` is the whole exploit. And the
 * "unguessable id" was a property of upload code nobody has written — a rule must
 * never rest on the shape of a file name that no code produces.
 *
 * ## What the real rules need, so this is not re-derived wrongly
 *
 * Storage rules cannot `get()` a Firestore document. "The upline who asked" is not a
 * fact available here; `request.auth.uid`, the object metadata and the path are. And
 * metadata is a dead end — the uploading client sets it, and `updateMetadata` is
 * itself a write, so an owner could rewrite their own grant afterwards. So the
 * identities the rule has to compare must live IN the path:
 *
 *   users/{uid}/profile/{fileName}           public read · owner write · image · 5 MB
 *   users/{uid}/transformations/{fileName}   public read · owner write · image · 8 MB
 *   proofs/{coachId}/{reviewerId}/{rest=**}  read: those two uids · write: coachId
 *
 * The public two keep the uid in the path because that is what makes the WRITE rule
 * enforceable. A uid is not a capability, and those files ride on the public report
 * card (F3) and portfolio (F9) by design.
 *
 * Proof media leaves `users/{uid}/` deliberately. Two of that prefix's three children
 * are world-readable, and one private folder inside a mostly-public prefix is exactly
 * how a future convenience rule — `match /users/{userId}/{allPaths=**}` with
 * `allow read: if true` — swallows evidence photos by accident. Splitting by
 * sensitivity at the top level means a mistake in the public branch cannot reach them.
 *
 * `proofs/{coachId}/{reviewerId}/…` reproduces the Firestore predicate with path
 * segments instead of a lookup: a proof's `coachId` and `requestedById` never change
 * after `requestProof` writes them, so a path built from that pair stays correct for
 * the life of the object. `{rest=**}` is there so the resize Function's derivative
 * matches too — a thumbnail no rule covers means the gallery serves originals, which
 * is the download egress §3 names as the dominant cost. The server hands the client
 * the path (it already owns proof authorization — D32), and the write rule still
 * checks `uid == coachId`, so a client that lies about the path cannot plant evidence
 * in someone else's folder.
 *
 * Land that in the SAME change as `storageBucket`, the uploader, and rules tests:
 * `initializeTestEnvironment` takes a `storage` block, so `e2e/rules.test.ts` can
 * finally assert "a coach outside the exchange cannot read the proof" — the day there
 * is a proof to read. Until then nothing needs a grant here. The size and contentType
 * bounds that were here were the genuinely useful part, and they lose nothing by
 * landing alongside the uploader that has to satisfy them.
 *
 * D33's EXIF re-encode stays a client-side guarantee in `image-client.ts` either way
 * (P4). Rules cannot inspect EXIF, and that does not change when Storage arrives.
 */
service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {

    // Nothing at all, until something in the app uploads. The note above says what
    // replaces this and what has to land in the same change.
    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
